00:39:36,090 --> 00:39:43,690
Rym: But no, we're talking about Easter eggs like the first Easter egg, which if you didn't know, it's actually the Atari video game adventure.

617
00:39:44,632 --> 00:39:45,421
Scott: Oh, really?

618
00:39:45,462 --> 00:39:46,210
Scott: That's the first one?

619
00:39:46,250 --> 00:39:49,250
Rym: That's the first one that was called an Easter egg.

620
00:39:49,290 --> 00:39:51,430
Scott: At least in software, or is it first one period?

621
00:39:51,590 --> 00:39:53,149
Rym: It's the first time anyone...

622
00:39:53,230 --> 00:39:53,898
Scott: I don't know about that.

623
00:39:54,343 --> 00:39:54,849
Scott: I don't think so.

624
00:39:55,130 --> 00:39:56,914
Rym: Well, the first time it was publicly released.

625
00:39:57,254 --> 00:40:02,405
Rym: The first known Easter egg, I just pulled out the Wikipedia to confirm, is in a 1973 video game, Moonlander.

626
00:40:06,772 --> 00:40:09,190
Scott: Okay, let me check and see something.

627
00:40:09,870 --> 00:40:17,350
Rym: Horizontally, you encounter a McDonald's restaurant, according to Wikipedia that I just opened a second ago.

628
00:40:18,911 --> 00:40:30,423
Rym: The term was coined in 1979 by Steve Wright, and the term was applied in 1980, when 1980 appears to be the first time.

629
00:40:30,443 --> 00:40:36,350
Rym: an Easter egg that nobody knew about except the person who put it in the game was discovered after the fact.

630
00:40:37,071 --> 00:40:39,735
Rym: And apparently, I just learned something I didn't know.

631
00:40:39,816 --> 00:40:48,470
Rym: According to Wikipedia, it was a big deal because that was before video game developers for Atari were allowed to have credits for their games.

632
00:40:48,810 --> 00:40:55,210
Rym: It was some anti-union, anti-unionization biz to make sure they couldn't negotiate for better salaries.

633
00:40:55,731 --> 00:41:05,645
Rym: So, Warren Robin Fett just fucking put his name in a secret room in Adventure, and then someone found it, and Atari was like, "Oh shit, we gotta recall the game,".

634
00:41:05,685 --> 00:41:08,910
Rym: and then they gave up because they realized that was impossible, and it was a whole thing.

635
00:41:10,871 --> 00:41:13,408
Scott: Alright, so what year did you claim this first...

636
00:41:13,750 --> 00:41:19,294
Rym: So, Wikipedia, the first one ever, Wikipedia claims 1973.

637
00:41:19,294 --> 00:41:22,154
Scott: Oh, when in 1973?

638
00:41:22,154 --> 00:41:22,596
Rym: I don't know.

639
00:41:22,616 --> 00:41:23,662
Rym: I'll have to click deeper.

640
00:41:23,782 --> 00:41:25,250
Rym: The game Moonlander.

641
00:41:26,110 --> 00:41:26,913
Scott: Okay, okay.

642
00:41:27,334 --> 00:41:31,607
Scott: So, it might be correct in that it defeated this, which I believe...

643
00:41:32,770 --> 00:41:33,918
Rym: February 25th, 1973, the game was dead complete.

644
00:41:35,730 --> 00:41:43,430
Scott: Okay, you have beaten what I believed to be an earlier easter egg by ten months, right?

645
00:41:44,050 --> 00:41:51,570
Scott: On December 7th, 1973, in the United Kingdom, Monty Python released a vinyl album.

646
00:41:51,771 --> 00:41:52,858
Rym: Oh, the third track!

647
00:41:53,080 --> 00:41:54,570
Rym: Did they call it an easter egg, though?

648
00:41:54,790 --> 00:42:03,550
Scott: The matching tie and handkerchief, and if you don't know, on this vinyl album, right, what they did is it was a three-sided album.

649
00:42:04,032 --> 00:42:08,430
Scott: So, on one side of the album, you would put the needle in the groove, and it would play.

650
00:42:08,610 --> 00:42:14,509
Scott: On the other side of the album, there were actually, like, double grooves, so...

651
00:42:14,931 --> 00:42:16,269
Rym: Like two interlocked spirals.

652
00:42:16,970 --> 00:42:26,969
Scott: Yes, so if you put the needle into one groove, it played side B, and if you put the needle in the other groove, it was like, you'd be like, "Wait a

653
00:42:26,989 --> 00:42:27,190
Scott: minute.".

654
00:42:27,691 --> 00:42:33,466
Scott: It's like, by accident, you would just hit the other groove, and it would play something completely different.

655
00:42:33,566 --> 00:42:34,910
Scott: It was a three-sided record.

656
00:42:35,390 --> 00:42:36,510
Rym: Something completely different, eh?

657
00:42:36,810 --> 00:42:37,751
Scott: Right, that's right.

658
00:42:37,872 --> 00:42:50,350
Scott: A hidden track, effectively, an entire hidden side to the entire album, content that you didn't know was there, which is, I argue, the exact definition of an easter egg.

659
00:42:51,131 --> 00:42:59,962
Scott: You were getting some sort of artwork, or some form of media, and there is something in it that you were not aware of.

660
00:43:00,022 --> 00:43:04,668
Scott: that required some secreting, or some digging, or something to uncover.

661
00:43:08,131 --> 00:43:17,370
Rym: Now, I think part of the difference, too, is the difference between what an easter egg is versus when people started calling this concept an easter egg specifically.

662
00:43:19,932 --> 00:43:23,130
Scott: Just because someone doesn't call it an easter egg doesn't mean it's not an easter egg.

663
00:43:23,290 --> 00:43:23,551
Rym: Exactly.

664
00:43:23,611 --> 00:43:31,550
Rym: I mean, I think certain hidden images in old tapestries, those are easter eggs.

665
00:43:33,751 --> 00:43:51,230
Rym: I think humans have had the concept of easter eggs and loved it and done it for as long as humans have made art, but I think sometime between, let's just say, 1970 and 1980, they started being called easter eggs, and that became the word for it, henceforth.

666
00:43:54,851 --> 00:44:07,890
Scott: And software especially, games mostly, but also other software, was ripe for having these, because in other art forms, there are only so many ways to hide things.

667
00:44:08,510 --> 00:44:13,921
Rym: Think about the complexity of printing a record that has a fiddly fucking third track hidden.

668
00:44:14,141 --> 00:44:16,045
Rym: Like, we had one, my dad had it.

669
00:44:16,426 --> 00:44:18,670
Rym: It was really hard to get that middle track to play.

670
00:44:18,891 --> 00:44:19,796
Scott: It was very hard.

671
00:44:19,856 --> 00:44:22,650
Scott: I almost didn't believe it was there, because I couldn't get it to go in this slot.

672
00:44:22,910 --> 00:44:24,420
Rym: We had to use the good turntable.

673
00:44:24,440 --> 00:44:25,790
Rym: It wouldn't work on my Big Bird one.

674
00:44:29,131 --> 00:44:44,210
Scott: But in other art forms, to get easter eggs in there, for them to either be non-obvious, so they're hidden enough that no one notices them, but also they're actually findable without going to extreme lengths.

675
00:44:45,711 --> 00:44:51,105
Scott: Something like, "Ah yes, if you shine a UV light on this, or wear special glasses, the secret message

676
00:44:51,145 --> 00:44:51,385
Scott: appears.".

677
00:44:51,406 --> 00:44:52,268
Scott: Who knows to do that?

678
00:44:52,308 --> 00:44:53,110
Scott: No one will ever see it.

679
00:44:53,331 --> 00:45:00,910
Rym: I feel like the best easter eggs are the ones that are so easy to find, or so obvious, yet no one notices them for a long time.

680
00:45:02,690 --> 00:45:03,854
Rym: That's the peak easter egg.

681
00:45:03,894 --> 00:45:05,459
Rym: That is S-tier easter egg.

682
00:45:05,860 --> 00:45:09,070
Rym: Once you know about it, it's so obvious you feel stupid.

683
00:45:10,751 --> 00:45:19,270
Scott: The sculpture that looks like a particular object, but if you look at it from a very specific angle, it lines up and it's a different object.

684
00:45:20,571 --> 00:45:24,790
Scott: Or there's those drawings where if you turn them upside down, it's something different.

685
00:45:27,171 --> 00:45:30,979
Rym: But software really gave you a lot of ways to do it, because one, there's the source code.

686
00:45:31,861 --> 00:45:36,270
Rym: There's easter eggs in source code, just like there's easter eggs in the spectrograms of audio.

687
00:45:36,330 --> 00:45:39,036
Scott: If you don't have access to the source code, it makes the easter eggs too easy to find.

688
00:45:39,056 --> 00:45:45,350
Scott: But if you don't have access to the source code, then it's like there's so many places to hide it.

689
00:45:47,651 --> 00:45:50,036
Scott: But the thing is, once someone knows, it's very easy.

690
00:45:50,356 --> 00:45:57,390
Scott: You just have to type in this keyboard shortcut that no one would ever press these 10 keys at the same time on this specific dialogue box.

691
00:45:57,890 --> 00:46:00,967
Scott: That would never happen, but once you know about it, you just do it.

692
00:46:01,027 --> 00:46:01,429
Scott: There it is.

693
00:46:01,670 --> 00:46:20,129
Rym: So supposedly, the first software easter egg, even if it wasn't called that, was actually October 1967 on the PDP-6, where if you used the make command and typed "make love", it would just respond "not war".

694
00:46:20,149 --> 00:46:21,330
Rym: And then it would work.

695
00:46:24,310 --> 00:46:29,360
Scott: There's also, I think, one of the most famous ones in gaming is Totaka's song.

696
00:46:29,981 --> 00:46:34,870
Scott: There's so many Nintendo games that either have it in a hidden or non-hidden fashion.

697
00:46:36,651 --> 00:46:51,890
Scott: Or there are also games that people have dug through the data of the games and found the song, but are not necessarily sure if there's actually a way to play it or not, legitimately, or if it's just on the disc or on the cartridge, taking up memory.

698
00:46:52,510 --> 00:46:55,333
Rym: There's also edge cases.

699
00:46:57,235 --> 00:47:04,863
Rym: If you play Mario 3, there's the 1-up sound, but there's actually an extended version of that, but it's not accessible via the game.

700
00:47:04,903 --> 00:47:06,044
Rym: That's not really an easter egg.

701
00:47:06,304 --> 00:47:12,210
Rym: It's just an artifact of how the game was programmed, because there's no way to access it except via a glitch, and it doesn't appear to have been intentional.

702
00:47:13,750 --> 00:47:15,398
Rym: The intentionality is really key.

703
00:47:15,418 --> 00:47:17,830
Rym: It has to be intentional, and it has to be accessible.

704
00:47:18,910 --> 00:47:25,960
Scott: I mean, Mario 3 is a great example because of what's a secret and what's an easter egg, right?

705
00:47:26,080 --> 00:47:31,388
Scott: It's ducking down on the white block and then going to get the whistle and then getting a white coin house.

706
00:47:31,428 --> 00:47:33,070
Scott: None of that stuff is in the instruction manual.

707
00:47:33,870 --> 00:47:35,717
Rym: But I would call that a secret.

708
00:47:35,817 --> 00:47:39,150
Rym: I haven't figured out what my line is yet, but that's definitely not an easter egg to me.

709
00:47:39,270 --> 00:47:40,492
Scott: It's in the strategy guide.

710
00:47:40,512 --> 00:47:42,515
Scott: They just tell you about all this stuff, right?

711
00:47:42,916 --> 00:47:45,460
Scott: But it's like, what's an easter egg and what's an easter egg?

712
00:47:45,480 --> 00:47:51,450
Scott: I think the easter egg, especially if it's a game, it's like there's functionality to those, right?

713
00:47:51,930 --> 00:47:53,913
Scott: A coin ship is giving you a bunch of coins, right?

714
00:47:53,933 --> 00:47:58,921
Scott: It's like part of the game somehow, even though it was just a hidden part of the game.

715
00:47:59,181 --> 00:48:04,569
Scott: Like a secret in Wolfenstein, you open up that door, there's a warp, there's ammo in there, there's treasure in there.

716
00:48:05,130 --> 00:48:09,157
Scott: But Easter eggs is literally just like, you know, a dev room.

717
00:48:09,377 --> 00:48:12,863
Scott: I think like in, uh, I think it's like a dev room.

718
00:48:12,943 --> 00:48:14,285
Scott: You go, it's just like a room.

719
00:48:14,766 --> 00:48:15,086
Scott: Oh no.

720
00:48:15,146 --> 00:48:16,829
Scott: Or system shock, I think as a dev room.

721
00:48:16,849 --> 00:48:17,230
Rym: I don't know.

722
00:48:17,710 --> 00:48:20,655
Scott: A lot of games have dev rooms where it's just a room.

723
00:48:20,695 --> 00:48:22,017
Scott: It doesn't do anything.

724
00:48:22,678 --> 00:48:29,890
Scott: It's just like a space where, you know, to that, where the sort of out of context leads me to a better definition for this part.

725
00:48:30,651 --> 00:48:35,876
Rym: I think Easter eggs have got to be not contextual to the media itself.

726
00:48:35,956 --> 00:48:38,319
Scott: Like they are not, but the Monty Python one is contextual.

727
00:48:38,359 --> 00:48:39,780
Scott: It's a whole, right?

728
00:48:40,101 --> 00:48:40,381
Rym: True.

729
00:48:40,721 --> 00:48:45,326
Rym: But like the adventure one is a credit appears on a screen.

730
00:48:45,346 --> 00:48:47,328
Rym: Like text appears saying, Hey, I made this game.

731
00:48:48,069 --> 00:48:48,449
Scott: That's true.

732
00:48:48,890 --> 00:48:49,010
Scott: Yep.

733
00:48:49,750 --> 00:48:53,173
Rym: So maybe the Monty Python one isn't technically an Easter egg.

734
00:48:54,474 --> 00:49:01,620
Rym: Maybe you could, the only thing that bothers me about Easter eggs is a lot of people will call things Easter eggs.

735
00:49:02,080 --> 00:49:06,543
Rym: that I would say are just references to other media in media.

736
00:49:07,084 --> 00:49:08,685
Rym: Like, Oh, it's an Easter egg.

737
00:49:08,725 --> 00:49:10,787
Rym: That's Spider-Man's web shooter.

738
00:49:10,807 --> 00:49:14,269
Rym: Like, no, that's just a reference to Spider-Man in a Spider-Man movie.

739
00:49:14,349 --> 00:49:15,330
Rym: Calm down nerd.

740
00:49:16,970 --> 00:49:21,714
Rym: Like I don't really consider things that are just references and movies and comics to be Easter eggs.

741
00:49:22,034 --> 00:49:26,298
Scott: The Easter egg would have to be like, yeah, those are just like cameos or whatever or homages, right?

742
00:49:26,318 --> 00:49:30,661
Scott: It actually has to be like a hidden, like a truly hidden thing, right?

743
00:49:30,681 --> 00:49:34,004
Scott: There has to be some step you take to like reveal it.

744
00:49:34,064 --> 00:49:34,324
Scott: Yeah.

745
00:49:34,664 --> 00:49:41,630
Rym: Like you take, you take page five of the comic and do the mad magazine thing in a particular spot.

746
00:49:42,030 --> 00:49:45,615
Rym: And Oh shit, there's a picture of Spider-Man, but this is not a Spider-Man comic.

747
00:49:45,655 --> 00:49:46,596
Rym: like their Easter egg.

748
00:49:46,616 --> 00:49:50,201
Rym: Yeah, I like them.

749
00:49:50,281 --> 00:49:54,026
Rym: I like, I like people hiding little treats around.

750
00:49:54,106 --> 00:49:54,266
Rym: Like.

751
00:49:54,306 --> 00:49:55,908
Rym: I just think it's a fun thing to do.

752
00:49:56,289 --> 00:49:57,150
Rym: It hurts no one.

753
00:49:58,350 --> 00:50:03,314
Rym: There's a lot of debate as I skim through this Wikipedia article of, are they okay?

754
00:50:03,734 --> 00:50:04,895
Rym: Are they security risks?

755
00:50:04,935 --> 00:50:07,638
Rym: Like there's people have a lot of opinions.

756
00:50:08,018 --> 00:50:08,778
Scott: Are they okay?

757
00:50:08,899 --> 00:50:10,380
Scott: If you're not hurting anyone, it's okay.

758
00:50:11,140 --> 00:50:11,400
Scott: Come on.

759
00:50:11,521 --> 00:50:13,262
Scott: That's the end of discussion.

760
00:50:13,422 --> 00:50:15,844
Rym: If the Easter egg is fascist, it's bad.

761
00:50:16,044 --> 00:50:16,845
Rym: Like full stop.

762
00:50:17,786 --> 00:50:17,906
Scott: Yeah.

763
00:50:17,926 --> 00:50:23,410
Scott: If you're hiding like secret, you know, a secret copy of Mein Kampf in your video game, then yes, that's bad.

764
00:50:23,630 --> 00:50:25,013
Rym: Ooh, the listener is a question.

765
00:50:25,373 --> 00:50:29,240
Rym: There are architect, uh, at a comment, architecturally strikes, like that's true.

766
00:50:29,340 --> 00:50:32,365
Rym: Walk through the right part of a building and like a thing happens.

767
00:50:32,726 --> 00:50:34,709
Rym: Step on the brick and King Todd winks at you.

768
00:50:35,730 --> 00:50:43,916
Scott: Uh, things like in grand central, it's like you whisper into the corner and the people on the other side can hear it, things like that.

769
00:50:43,936 --> 00:50:48,200
Scott: where it's like, that's really more, you know, it's like a hidden feature, right?

770
00:50:48,240 --> 00:50:54,544
Scott: Where it's like, it's just, you know, there's something about it, but you just don't, you just don't know.

771
00:50:54,724 --> 00:50:58,667
Scott: unless it has been, you know, it just looks like a normal object, right?

772
00:50:58,807 --> 00:51:02,410
Scott: It looks just like a room, but actually there's something in this room that you can't see.

773
00:51:02,750 --> 00:51:19,390
Rym: I still think it, it's only an Easter egg if it is intentional by the creator and intentionally hidden by the creator, because I think a key component for me of what, when I viscerally feel something is an Easter egg is when I find it or I find out about it.

774
00:51:20,311 --> 00:51:22,353
Rym: My first reaction is, ha, wow.

775
00:51:22,493 --> 00:51:25,216
Rym: Like I'm surprised and slightly delighted.

776
00:51:25,456 --> 00:51:29,840
Rym: Like, like I'm, I, there's always a little bit of excitement like, Oh, what's this?

777
00:51:29,860 --> 00:51:31,222
Rym: Ooh, right now.

778
00:51:31,242 --> 00:51:39,150
Scott: Like imagine if someone had, you know, an old slidey bookshelf with a secret room, is that an Easter egg, you know, or is it, is it too far?

779
00:51:39,330 --> 00:51:39,550
Scott: Right.

780
00:51:39,970 --> 00:51:44,156
Scott: But for example, I was at Disneyland in November, right?

781
00:51:44,576 --> 00:51:48,981
Scott: And we were going through, you know, in, uh, I don't know how it is.

782
00:51:49,001 --> 00:51:55,930
Scott: I haven't been to Disney world this millennium, but I remember when I went to Disney world as a kid, I wanted to go in the castle and that's not really a thing.

783
00:51:56,651 --> 00:52:04,999
Scott: Uh, but in Disneyland, there was something that approached going in the castle, basically behind the castle to the left, right?

784
00:52:05,360 --> 00:52:13,328
Scott: There was a shop where you could buy princess dresses, um, of all the different princesses, uh, like a fancy dress shop, right?

785
00:52:13,348 --> 00:52:15,310
Scott: Like you want the high quality costume, right?

786
00:52:15,891 --> 00:52:21,716
Scott: And then next to it was like a little, a door, a port, a doorway, and basically it was up a staircase.

787
00:52:21,736 --> 00:52:23,999
Scott: I don't think it's a very accessible area with the staircase.

788
00:52:24,059 --> 00:52:25,961
Scott: There was no other way to get in there besides stairs.

789
00:52:26,661 --> 00:52:35,350
Scott: And it was just a short walkthrough where you went in one side and it came out the other and you just go in through stairs and it looked like you were in a castle.

790
00:52:35,410 --> 00:52:46,142
Scott: It was castle inside Dunjany themed and it just sorta had little scenes, dioramas just showing you the Sleeping Beauty story because it's Sleeping Beauty's castle in California.

791
00:52:46,802 --> 00:52:49,045
Scott: And you know, that, that was it, right?

792
00:52:49,065 --> 00:52:50,506
Scott: It was just walkthrough.

793
00:52:51,527 --> 00:52:54,090
Scott: There's the story of Sleeping Beauty and a few thingies, right?

794
00:52:54,130 --> 00:52:55,583
Scott: I think that leads to another aspect.

795
00:52:58,590 --> 00:53:09,800
Scott: But the, the Easter egg part was that while I was walking through, right, there was, you know, it was decorated like the inside of a castle and there was a wooden door, right?

796
00:53:10,060 --> 00:53:13,062
Scott: Like a decorative, you know, castle Dunjany wooden door.

797
00:53:13,843 --> 00:53:17,746
Scott: And I pulled on the handle to be like, "Lol, I'm going to try to open the dungeon

798
00:53:17,786 --> 00:53:18,066
Scott: door.".

799
00:53:18,687 --> 00:53:21,609
Scott: The door shook and there was a, like,

800
00:53:21,649 --> 00:53:22,050
Scott: "Hahaha.".

801
00:53:22,854 --> 00:53:23,278
Rym: That's good.

802
00:53:23,379 --> 00:53:24,187
Rym: That might be an Easter egg.

803
00:53:25,011 --> 00:53:25,311
Scott: Right.

804
00:53:25,632 --> 00:53:31,459
Scott: And so it's like, you know, it's like, it wasn't that hidden because it's like you're just opening a door.

805
00:53:31,620 --> 00:53:32,621
Scott: that's a door.

806
00:53:32,661 --> 00:53:34,003
Scott: It's not hidden, right?

807
00:53:34,403 --> 00:53:38,389
Scott: But it's like nothing in there would make you think that anything was interactive.

808
00:53:38,469 --> 00:53:39,430
Scott: It was just a walkthrough.

809
00:53:39,710 --> 00:53:40,712
Rym: But I think that's it too.

810
00:53:40,752 --> 00:53:48,044
Rym: It has, it not only has to be hidden and intentional, they have to want people to find it.

811
00:53:49,546 --> 00:53:51,910
Rym: If you don't want people to find it, it's not an Easter egg.

812
00:53:55,511 --> 00:53:55,832
Rym: All right.

813
00:53:56,413 --> 00:54:03,626
Rym: We're discussing, actually, there's a lively chat on this one because this is a hot dog sandwich situation.

814
00:54:03,666 --> 00:54:05,790
Rym: This is, it's actually really hard to define.

815
00:54:05,850 --> 00:54:09,789
Scott: It is one of those cases where, yeah, it's like we could argue about the definition all day.

816
00:54:10,811 --> 00:54:14,476
Scott: But who really cares, right, of classifying what is and what is not an Easter egg.

817
00:54:14,876 --> 00:54:23,367
Scott: The point is, is that when there are things hidden with whimsy and, you know, joy and serendipity.

818
00:54:26,690 --> 00:54:27,770
Rym: Serendipitous discovery.

819
00:54:28,630 --> 00:54:28,751
Scott: Yes.

820
00:54:29,152 --> 00:54:33,150
Scott: Those are things that make people happy as long as you don't overdo it.

821
00:54:33,350 --> 00:54:39,098
Rym: So case in point, imagine before anyone, like everyone knew about the third track and understood it.

822
00:54:39,859 --> 00:54:48,770
Rym: Imagine you put the record, you play that record and you play that record and one day you put the needle down and it happens to hit that other groove you didn't know about.

823
00:54:49,311 --> 00:54:51,795
Rym: And a record you've listened to before plays something different.

824
00:54:52,517 --> 00:54:57,727
Rym: Imagine what that feels like for 30 seconds before you figure out what's going on.

825
00:54:57,767 --> 00:54:59,530
Rym: Like that is sublime.

826
00:55:00,610 --> 00:55:01,351
Scott: That's the key.

827
00:55:01,452 --> 00:55:06,539
Scott: It doesn't really matter what your definition is, whether it counts as an Easter egg or not, who cares, right?

828
00:55:06,579 --> 00:55:14,750
Scott: The point is there are certain things along these lines that can make people feel good, positive, right?

829
00:55:15,370 --> 00:55:16,192
Scott: Good, right?

830
00:55:16,212 --> 00:55:16,794
Scott: We like them.

831
00:55:17,135 --> 00:55:17,817
Scott: We enjoy them.

832
00:55:18,118 --> 00:55:22,710
Scott: They make the world nicer for humans to live in when they're experiencing artwork.

833
00:55:23,371 --> 00:55:25,115
Scott: And that is a good thing.

834
00:55:25,196 --> 00:55:31,330
Scott: So if you can do a thing like that in your artwork successfully, you know, in a positive way that works.

835
00:55:32,891 --> 00:55:42,850
Scott: If someone wants to say it's not an Easter egg or it is whatever definition who gives a shit, as long as it is achieving the positive ends, we like that a lot.

836
00:55:43,751 --> 00:55:55,430
Scott: And if anyone has more cool examples, because maybe younger or older than us and you know better or worse than us, share so that we can all have that feeling some more.

837
00:56:01,020 --> 00:56:03,390
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

838
00:56:03,450 --> 00:56:05,960
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

839
00:56:06,160 --> 00:56:12,200
Scott: Be sure to visit our website at frontrowcrew.com for show notes, discussion, links and more.

840
00:56:12,661 --> 00:56:15,345
Rym: Remember, GeekNights is not one but four different shows.

841
00:56:15,646 --> 00:56:17,309
Rym: Mondays are science and technology.

842
00:56:17,349 --> 00:56:19,092
Rym: Tuesdays, gaming and games.

843
00:56:19,212 --> 00:56:21,035
Rym: Wednesdays are animation and comics.

844
00:56:21,356 --> 00:56:23,840
Rym: Thursdays are arbitrary and indiscriminate.

845
00:56:24,560 --> 00:56:29,380
Rym: GeekNights is distributed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 international license.

846
00:56:29,940 --> 00:56:34,210
Scott: GeekNights is recorded live with no studio and no audience.

847
00:56:34,692 --> 00:56:38,160
Scott: But unlike those other late shows, it's actually recorded at night.

848
00:56:38,621 --> 00:56:55,080
Rym: And the Patreon patrons for this episode of GeekNights are Alan Joyce, Linki G, Dre, Lily Tenderberry, Kace, Cara Makes the Music, Chris the Doc, Chris Ferrar, Clinton Welton, Finn, Joel Hayes, Penny Reimer, Rebecca Dunne, Sam Erickson, Shervin Von Harl, and a bunch of people whose names I will not say.

849
00:56:56,480 --> 00:56:59,708
Rym: Probably not going to do a show on Monday because I'll still be in Ra Cha Cha.

850
00:56:59,728 --> 00:57:01,773
Rym: I hope you guys enjoy the eclipse.

851
00:57:02,295 --> 00:57:04,400
Rym: Absolutely do not risk your eyes.

852
00:57:05,060 --> 00:57:11,220
Rym: Follow whatever advice you need to follow to test whatever glasses you might use to look at that thing if you're in its path.

853
00:57:11,880 --> 00:57:17,859
Rym: And tonight, I will leave you with... Something's going on, just look around.

854
00:57:20,400 --> 00:57:21,898
Rym: Fear is on their eyes.

855
00:57:22,500 --> 00:57:24,520
Scott: And there's blood all over the ground.

856
00:57:24,680 --> 00:57:27,080
Scott: Let's all just blindfold the poor.

857
00:57:27,481 --> 00:57:30,099
Scott: We must remind them what's in store.

858
00:57:30,940 --> 00:57:34,299
Rym: We got 'em now, just break 'em down a little bit more.

859
00:57:36,787 --> 00:57:38,839
Scott: I said hey, you, feed the machine.

860
00:57:39,380 --> 00:57:42,900
Scott: Bring 'em all back down to their knees, no time to waste.

861
00:57:43,322 --> 00:57:46,920
Rym: Remind the slate, they ain't gonna make it out alive today.

862
00:57:47,120 --> 00:57:49,760
Rym: I said hey, you, poison the well.

863
00:57:50,340 --> 00:57:52,380
Rym: Watch it all burn, take 'em straight to hell.

864
00:57:52,560 --> 00:57:55,320
Rym: He's got the whole world in his hands.

865
00:57:55,581 --> 00:57:56,697
Scott: It was nice to know ya.

866
00:57:57,180 --> 00:57:58,498
Scott: Bob, Ben, Dan, come on!

